@3.5 Arrays
.
=3.5 ARRAYS
.
=SYNTAX
.
:GP.ARRPTR(a())
.
=WHAT IT COSTS
.
.  ASM: GP.ARRPTR
.    ASM -- a keyword, run by machine code in the GP runtime block. A program
.    using ANY GP keyword pays for the whole block.
.
=DESCRIPTION
.
.  | Sorting is SORT.INC.BL (SEC 4.7), not a keyword: 408 bytes of the GP
.  | block, which is all or nothing, carried by every program that never
.  | sorted anything. GP.ARRPTR is here because the module is built on it --
.  | a BASL subroutine cannot be handed an array, so an address is the only
.  | interface there is.
.
.GP.ARRPTR returns the address of element zero -- the header is already
.skipped -- so machine code reached by GP.CALL can work on the array in bulk.
.Stride is yours to add: 2 bytes per element for a string array (each element
.is a pointer to the string's block -- follow it, and see GP.STRPTR for the
.layout), 6 for a numeric one. Multi-dimensional arrays are rejected, and
.GP.ARRPTR(A(3)) is a syntax error -- add 3*2 or 3*6 yourself.
.
=EXAMPLE PROGRAM
.
.  ARRAYS.EXP.BL -- runnable, in the GPC-BASIC folder
.
=SEE ALSO
.
>36|4.7 SORT.INC.BL -- shell sort a string array
>36|4.7 SORT.INC.BL -- shell sort a string array
